{
 "item": "R9",
 "where": "Lemma 6.18 (proof)",
 "paper text": [
  {
   "where": "Lemma 6.18 (proof)",
   "text": "By~\\eqref{r1:availability}, $ce$ is an edge or is supplied by a\ncomponent outside $\\Psi_3$. Since $e$ is not an attachment of $B$,\nshortening the corresponding $c$--$e$ path to $ce$ gives\n$(\\Psi_3\\cup B)+ce$ as a minor of $G$. Consider a torus embedding\nof this minor. In a genus-one rotation of $\\Psi_3+ce$, the\nsequences on $b,c,d$ along facial walks meeting all three are\n$bcd$ and $bcdc$. For a planar rotation,\nno boundary walk contains all three attachments, and the only possible\nannuli are $bc\\mid ad$ and $bc\\mid de$~\\supp{R9}. In each disk sequence\n$b,c$ are consecutive, and in each annulus they form one boundary\nsequence. Thus Lemmas~\\ref{cor:boundary-completions}\nand~\\ref{lem:annulus-embedding} require $B+bc$ to be planar in every\ncase. The minor is therefore nontoroidal, so by minimality it is $G$."
  }
 ],
 "what": "Every rotation system of Psi_3 + ce. Attachments a, b, c, d, e; twins omitted.",
 "how to read": "At each vertex, 'cyclic orders' lists the neighbor orders lexicographically, starting each order with the least neighbor. Rotation systems are numbered from 0 in the order of 'vertices', with the last vertex varying fastest. The entry rows[i] indexes 'distinct rows' for rotation system i. Each row gives the genus (2 - V + E - F)/2 and the sorted attachment sequences of the facial walks. After u->v a walk takes v->w, where w follows u at v. A sequence records every visit to an attachment, starting at the least cyclic shift and keeping the walk direction. For claims up to reversal, take the least cyclic shift of either direction.",
